### Quickstart



```
import Ravensight from 'ravensight';

const rs = new Ravensight({
  ingestKey: 'gt_live_your_publishable_key',
  gameVersion: '1.4.0',
});

// Reads the server kill switch and opens a session. Call it once at startup.
await rs.init();

rs.track('level_completed', { level: 3, deaths: 2, seconds: 91 });

// Optional: player feedback from an in-game form.
await rs.submitFeedback('The boss fight is too long', 'suggestion', 4);
```



In a browser the SDK sends `game_started` and `game_exited` for you; pass `autoEvents: false` to send your own.



In CommonJS, use `const Ravensight = require('ravensight');`. Events go out in batches on a timer and when the page is hidden, so you never have to call `flush()` yourself. A Node process should `await rs.dispose()` before it exits, so the last batch goes out.

### Node servers with many players



The server takes the device id from the session, so every device needs its own session. The SDK keeps one per device id for you. On a game server, create one client and pass each player's id as the third argument to `track()`: a pseudonymous id your game generates, never an email or account name. Node has no `localStorage`, so pass an explicit `MemoryStorage` to keep the server's own id out of any shared storage.



```
import Ravensight, { MemoryStorage } from 'ravensight';

const rs = new Ravensight({
  ingestKey: process.env.RAVENSIGHT_KEY,
  gameVersion: '1.4.0',
  platform: 'dedicated-server',
  storage: new MemoryStorage(),
});
await rs.init();

function onMatchFinished(player, match) {
  rs.track('match_finished', { mode: match.mode, seconds: match.seconds }, { deviceId: player.analyticsId });
}

// On shutdown, the last flush for every player at once.
process.on('SIGTERM', async () => {
  await rs.dispose({ timeoutMs: 5000 });
  process.exit(0);
});
```



- **One request is one device.** A flush sends the run of queued events that share the device id at the head of the queue, up to 50, then the next run. Interleaved players cost more requests than one player, but nothing is ever sent under the wrong id.

- **Sessions are cached per id**, up to `maxSessions`. Past that the least recently used is dropped and reopens on that player's next event. Raise it if more players than that are active at once.

- **Limits are per game, shared by every player** on the client: 60 session creations and 600 tracked events a minute. A burst past that gets a `429`, and the SDK holds the events and retries. See [Usage, billing and limits](https://www.ravensight.io/docs/#limits).

- **`dispose()` is the last flush.** Call it on shutdown. Anything it cannot send inside `timeoutMs` is counted as dropped.

### Joining the browser's session



When the same player also runs the browser SDK, the browser can hand its session to your server, so server-side events land in the same analytics session as the player's own, under the browser's device id.



```
// Browser: send the token to your server with your own request.
await fetch('/api/link-analytics', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify({ rsToken: rs.sessionToken }),
});

// Server: one call per player, before that player's next event.
rs.joinSession(body.rsToken, { deviceId: player.analyticsId });
rs.track('purchase_verified', { sku: 'gold_pack' }, { deviceId: player.analyticsId });
```



The next session opened for that id sends the token once as `joinToken`. The server answers with a session of its own that shares the browser's session id and device id, and never lives longer than the browser's. The local id is only the key the SDK caches that session under. If the server refuses the token, the SDK opens a plain session instead and records `invalid_join_token` in `stats.lastError`. Only a live session of the same game can be joined. A join from a playtest session stays synthetic, so the game server's events are marked as the playtest's too.

### Privacy: two controls that do different things



`setTrackingEnabled(false)` is the player's choice. It is saved in `UserDefaults` before the call returns, works before `start`, clears the queue and stops every request on this launch and every later one. `stopAllTelemetry()` is a stop for this run only: nothing is saved, the queue and the persisted queue file are cleared, `track` returns false, and feedback and reports are refused. The difference that matters is the playtest run: a synthetic session is driven by an agent on a test build, not by a player, so it ignores the saved opt-out, and only `stopAllTelemetry()` silences it.



| Call | Saved | Normal session | Synthetic (playtest) session |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `setTrackingEnabled(false)` | Yes | Queue cleared, no request of any kind, now and on every later launch. | Ignored, with one console line. The run carries on. |
| `stopAllTelemetry()` | No | Everything stops for this run. The next launch starts normally. `resumeTelemetry()` undoes it, and a saved opt-out still wins. | The run stops sending. |
| `resetDeviceId()` | Yes | A new durable id is saved and returned, the queue is cleared and the session ends. | A new durable id is saved for the next normal launch. The run keeps its playtest id. |



An analytics switch in your settings screen uses both:



```
func setAnalytics(_ on: Bool) {
    Ravensight.setTrackingEnabled(on)     // saved: holds on every later launch
    if on {
        Ravensight.resumeTelemetry()
    } else {
        Ravensight.stopAllTelemetry()     // also stops a playtest run, right now
    }
}
```

### Player Reports with a screenshot



`submitReport` sends what a player wrote about a problem, with your own metadata and, only if you pass one, a screenshot. The SDK never captures the screen: capture it yourself after the player chose to attach it, with anything private hidden. On macOS pass `screenshotData:` with JPEG, PNG or WebP bytes. Reports land in the dashboard's Player Reports inbox.



```
// A failed report kept for a retry, with the session it was sent under.
var pendingReport: (report: RavensightReport, session: String?)?

// Build the report once: its clientId, capture time and screenshot bytes
// are fixed here, so resubmitting this value sends the same submission.
let report = RavensightReport(
    category: "bug",
    message: "The ferry never arrives",
    screenshot: capturedImage,          // UIImage?, nil for a text report
    metadata: ["zone": "harbour", "attempt": 3]
)
let session = Ravensight.sessionToken

Ravensight.submitReport(report) { result in
    switch result {
    case .success(let receipt):
        print("report \(receipt.reportId ?? receipt.id) saved")
    case .failure(let error)
        where error.code == "upload_failed" || error.code == "report_processing" || error.status == 0:
        // Retry later with the same value, and only while the session is the same one.
        pendingReport = (report, session)
    case .failure(let error):
        print("report refused: \(error.code)")
    }
}

// Later:
if let pending = pendingReport, pending.session != nil, pending.session == Ravensight.sessionToken {
    Ravensight.submitReport(pending.report) { _ in }
}
```



**What it costs.** A text report counts as 1 event on the shared event meter. A screenshot adds 20,000 events, reserved when the report is accepted, which is about $0.01 once the included events are spent. The SDK makes one attempt per step and never retries a report on its own, because a screenshot reserves usage.



**When to retry.** The server recognises a resubmission by the report's `clientId` within one session only. Retry only after `upload_failed`, `report_processing` or a status of 0, with the same `RavensightReport` value and the same session token as the failed attempt: the server finds the existing submission and the SDK carries on from where it stopped. A retry under a different session (after a session expiry, a timeout in the background, `resetDeviceId` or a relaunch) is a new submission, stored again, and a screenshot reserves its events again. The server allows two upload attempts per submission and releases an unfinished screenshot report after 15 minutes. Limits are checked on the device first: a message of 1 to 500 characters, a screenshot of at most 1,000,000 bytes and 4,000,000 pixels, scaled to fit 1600 by 1600. `submitFeedback` stays as the lighter text only call.

### Queued, accepted and delivered



`track` returning `true` means the event is in the queue on the device, nothing more. Delivery is reported separately: `Ravensight.stats` counts `queued`, `delivered` (events in batches the server answered `202` for), `pending`, `droppedEvents` and `restored`; `lastDelivery` is `.accepted`, `.failed(status:)` or `.offline`; and `onFlush` is called on the main queue after every request that carried events. The server's own `accepted` count in that `202` answer can be lower than the batch the SDK counts as delivered, because an event timestamped more than 90 days ago is dropped without failing the request (see the [HTTP API](https://www.ravensight.io/docs/#http-api)). Accepted events reach your reports after the server's insert buffer flushes, which takes a few seconds.



```
Ravensight.shared?.onFlush = { outcome in
    if outcome.succeeded {
        print("delivered \(outcome.sent), \(outcome.pending) still queued")
    } else {
        print("not delivered (status \(outcome.status)), will retry")
    }
}
```

### GET /api/v1/settings



Reads the tracking kill switch, and names the game the key belongs to, so a client or the doctor can confirm it ships the key for the game and environment it meant. Auth: `X-API-Key`.



Response, 200



```
{
  "trackingEnabled": true,
  "game": { "slug": "nebula-drifter", "name": "Nebula Drifter", "environment": "production" }
}
```



`slug` is the key's own game id, never another game's, and the answer never includes the key or its hash.

### Worked examples



Each example is one session, read under the default detection profile (a death is a failure, rage quit on, no terminal events), with every event inside the queried window. The outcome is what the code computes, word for word where it writes an indicator.



#### Level start



```
1  game_started    {}
2  level_started   {"level": "cavern-1"}
3  boss_entered    {"level": "cavern-1"}
4  shop_entered    {"level": "cavern-1", "rs_kind": "none"}
5  level_started   {}
```



The level funnel shows `cavern-1` with 2 starts: line 2, and line 3, because `boss_entered` contains `enter`. Line 4 would match too, but `rs_kind: "none"` opts it out. Lines 1 and 5 are level starts by name (`game_started` contains `start`) but carry no place, so the funnel never sees them. With no completion, `cavern-1` reads 0 percent complete.



#### Level complete



```
1  level_started   {"level": "1-1"}
2  level_complete  {"level": "1-1"}
3  level_started   {"track": "1-2"}
4  stage_cleared   {"location": "1-2"}
5  window_opened   {"level": "1-2"}
```



`1-1`: 1 start, 1 completion, 100 percent. `1-2`: 1 start (line 3, where `track` is the place) and 2 completions, line 4 (`cleared` contains `clear`, and `location` is the place) and line 5, because `window_opened` contains `win`. Two completions against one start is a completion rate of 2, so send line 5 with `rs_kind: "none"`.



#### Death



```
1  level_started   {"level": "boss"}
2  player_died     {"level": "boss"}
3  ship_destroyed  {"level": "boss"}
4  enemy_killed    {"level": "boss"}
5  run_ended       {"level": "boss", "rs_kind": "death"}
```



The session's death count is 4, and the funnel shows `boss` with 1 start, 0 completions and 4 deaths: lines 2, 3 and 4 by name, line 5 by its `rs_kind`. Line 4 is the trap: `enemy_killed` contains `killed`, so an enemy's death counts as the player's unless it is sent with `rs_kind: "none"`. The rage quit rules read only `died` and `destroyed` names (plus `rs_kind: "death"`), so for them lines 2, 3 and 5 are deaths and line 4 is not; that is 3 deaths in the last 5 events and 3 at `boss`, so this session is also flagged rage quit likely.



#### Rage quit



```
1  level_started   {"level": "3"}
2  player_died     {}
3  player_died     {}
4  hint_shown      {}
5  player_died     {}
```



Flagged rage quit likely with the indicator `3 deaths in last 5 events`. The same place rule does not fire: deaths with no place are left out of it, so a placeless death is never counted as being at any place. Sent with `{"level": "3"}`, the same three deaths would add `3 deaths at same location (3)`. The last event is a death whose name contains `died`, so a query whose window ends more than 10 minutes after line 5 also adds `Session ended immediately after death (Nmin ago)`, with N the whole minutes since that event; a last event named `ship_destroyed` would not, because that rule reads only `died` names and `rs_kind: "death"`.



#### Stuck



```
1  level_started   {"level": "ledge"}
2  jump_failed     {"level": "ledge"}   sent 10 times
3  hint_shown      {}
4  jump_failed     {}                   sent 4 times
5  game_exited     {}
```



17 events in all. Flagged potentially stuck with the indicator `Repeating same action: jump_failed (10x)`. The rule is one event name repeated 10 or more times at one place, in a session of fewer than 20 events (19 at most). Repeats are counted per event name and place, and events with no place share one group of their own: the 4 placeless `jump_failed` on line 4 are counted apart from the 10 at `ledge`, and had all 14 been sent without a place they would have counted as 14 at that one placeless group. Three more events of any kind would make 20, and the session would not be flagged.
